{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Pensacola's second rail approach, built east across the Florida Panhandle toward Jacksonville -- the direct ancestor of the Gulf Coast corridor the L&N's Gulf Wind later ran (see louisville_and_nashville_gulf_wind.geojson, whose modeled geometry currently only covers the New Orleans-Mobile portion revived by Amtrak's 2025 Mardi Gras Service; this file picks up the story east of Pensacola). The Pensacola and Atlantic Railroad was incorporated by the Florida Legislature March 4 1881, to run about 160 miles from Pensacola to the Apalachicola River near Chattahoochee. The L&N took financial control just weeks later (May 9 1881, buying most of its stock and all its bonds) even before construction was complete. The line reached across Pensacola Bay on a new railroad bridge, first crossed by passengers Aug 15 1882; the Apalachicola River extension was completed Feb 10 1883, and the line reached all the way to Jacksonville on April 11 1883. The Pensacola and Atlantic ceased operating as an independent company July 1 1885, folded fully into the L&N system. Geometry follows the railroad's own documented station list across the Panhandle (Pensacola-Milton-Crestview-DeFuniak Springs-Bonifay-Chipley-Cottondale-Marianna-Chattahoochee-Tallahassee-Jacksonville) -- several of these towns (Crestview, DeFuniak Springs, Bonifay, Chipley, Cottondale) were themselves founded directly along the railroad's path. Straight-line segments between those documented stations, NOT digitized from period track maps; the Chattahoochee-Tallahassee-Jacksonville stretch beyond Marianna is less precisely sourced than the Pensacola-Marianna portion.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pensacola_and_Atlantic_Railroad and https://wfrm.org/history/the-pa/ (incorporated March 4 1881 to run Pensacola to the Apalachicola River near Chattahoochee, ~160 miles; L&N took control of stock/bonds May 9 1881; Pensacola Bay bridge first crossed by passengers Aug 15 1882; Apalachicola River extension complete Feb 10 1883; Jacksonville reached April 11 1883; ceased independent operation July 1 1885, absorbed into L&N)", "https://twoegg.blogspot.com/2017/02/jacksonrailroad.html and https://pulsegulfcoast.com/historic-train-stations-of-the-gulf-coast/ (station-by-station construction account: Escambia Bay bridge Aug 1882, then Milton, the Yellow River crossing, Crestview -- named for its 1891 L&N depot -- DeFuniak Springs -- founded by and named for P&A vice president Frederick R. De Funiak -- Bonifay, Chipley, Cottondale, Marianna, and the Apalachicola River bridge to Chattahoochee, finished May 1883)"], "not_yet_sourced": "Whether Tallahassee was a scheduled stop on the original P&A route versus a nearby waypoint used to approximate the curve toward Jacksonville; precise frequency for any era, all estimated; exact discontinuation date for through Pensacola-Jacksonville passenger service (not confirmed to be the same as the Gulf Wind's 1971 Amtrak-transition end date modeled in that separate file).", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Pensacola and Atlantic Railroad", "line": "Pensacola and Atlantic: Pensacola \u2013 Jacksonville", "id": "pensacola-atlantic-railroad", "color": "#7A4419", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 35, "speed_source": "estimated: early 1880s steam-era railroad; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1883, "end": 1885, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Pensacola Bay bridge first crossed by passengers Aug 15 1882; extended to the Apalachicola River near Chattahoochee Feb 10 1883, then all the way to Jacksonville April 11 1883; frequency estimated/unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pensacola_and_Atlantic_Railroad", "https://wfrm.org/history/the-pa/", "https://twoegg.blogspot.com/2017/02/jacksonrailroad.html", "https://pulsegulfcoast.com/historic-train-stations-of-the-gulf-coast/"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-87.2169, 30.4213], "to": [-87.0353, 30.6216], "chord_mi": 17.55, "traced_mi": 20.36, "snap_m": [231, 138], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["P&A Subdivision (19.4 mi)", "CSX PD Subdivision (0.96 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-87.0353, 30.6216], "to": [-86.5703, 30.7619], "chord_mi": 29.29, "traced_mi": 30.37, "snap_m": [138, 434], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["P&A Subdivision (30.37 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-86.5703, 30.7619], "to": [-86.1147, 30.7205], "chord_mi": 27.23, "traced_mi": 28.6, "snap_m": [434, 44], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["P&A Subdivision (28.51 mi)", "DeFuniak Springs Siding (0.09 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-86.1147, 30.7205], "to": [-85.6802, 30.7924], "chord_mi": 26.3, "traced_mi": 29.5, "snap_m": [44, 165], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["P&A Subdivision (29.23 mi)", "RailUSA [disused] (0.18 mi)", "DeFuniak Springs Siding (0.09 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-85.5416, 30.7